# Line endings, pinned per file type.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. For a POSIX shell script that is fatal rather than cosmetic: Git Bash reports
# "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" and the wrapper never runs, which on a pit laptop looks like a
# broken project rather than a line-ending setting. CI does not catch it because the hosted
# Windows runners do not use that default.
#
# Deliberately not `* text=auto`: that would renormalize the whole tree in one commit and bury
# real history. Only the files whose endings actually change behavior are pinned.

# Shell scripts must stay LF on every platform, including Windows.
gradlew      text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image — cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.bat        text eol=crlf
*.cmd        text eol=crlf

# Binary, never touched.
*.jar        binary
*.wpilog     binary
*.kdb        binary
*.pdf        binary
*.vsix       binary
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.ico        binary
graphify-out/graph.json merge=graphify
